  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "American contractor Bob Donaldson organised large building projects around clearly separated responsibilities. Estimating, sales, purchasing and operations each had their own departments. For major assignments, an account executive worked with a project manager and assistant, while field supervisors and foremen monitored installation quality and productivity.\n\nHe treated payment terms as part of managing the work itself. Before starting a contract, he wanted the billing schedule and approval procedures settled. Changes needed written instructions and prompt invoicing. Monthly cash-flow meetings brought finance and operations staff together, so the people directing construction also understood the demands on working capital.",
    "html": "<p>American contractor Bob Donaldson organised large building projects around clearly separated responsibilities. Estimating, sales, purchasing and operations each had their own departments. For major assignments, an account executive worked with a project manager and assistant, while field supervisors and foremen monitored installation quality and productivity.</p>\r\n<p>He treated payment terms as part of managing the work itself. Before starting a contract, he wanted the billing schedule and approval procedures settled. Changes needed written instructions and prompt invoicing. Monthly cash-flow meetings brought finance and operations staff together, so the people directing construction also understood the demands on working capital.</p>\r\n",
    "word_count": 97,
